<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="section-errors" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Hypothesis Tests and Error Types</title>
  <p> 
    I have now developed two methods of inferential statistics:
    hypothesis testing and confidence intervals. I also have developed
    some mathematical theory from which I can calculate confidence
    intervals, <m>z</m>-scores and <m>p</m>-values. In this section, I
    want to step back a bit an talk about this whole process. I'll
    introduce some important terminology and also talk about how
    researchers make the important choice of a threshold of
    significance. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Hypothesis testing is about determining significance of a sample
    and its sample statistic. This is probabilistic: we want something
    to be true, but the best we can do is conclude that something is
    likely. As we are dealing with probability, there is always the
    potential for error: something unlikely can still happen. I want
    to understand the details of these potential errors, so this is a
    section about error. 
  </p>
  <p>
    As mentioned before, all of this probability relies of having good
    data in the first place. This section makes the same assumption
    that we actually have good data. If the data is biased or
    otherwise comprimised, then none of the mathematical discussion
    helps at all. In this case, there are more fundamental problems
    with the research study which would have to be solved before even
    considering any of the current analysis.
  </p>
  <p>
    Let me start with the big definition of this section. 
  </p>
  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        The context of these error is hypothesis testing and
        significance. In this context, there are two major kind of
        error. 
        <ul>
          <li>
            A <term>Type 1 Error</term> happens when we conclude that
            our data is signficant and we reject the null hypothesis,
            but in truth the null hypotheses is actually true in the
            population. This is also called a <term>False
            Positive</term>, since we have a positive result
            (significance) but it is not a true result. 
          </li>
          <li>
            A <term>Type 2 Error</term> happens when we conclude that
            our data is not significant and we cannot reject the null
            hypotehsis, but in truth the null hypothesis is actually
            false in the population. This is also called a <term>False
            Negative</term>, since we have a negative result (no
            significane) but it is not a true result.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <p>
    A common way to explain these errors is in terms of criminal
    trials. The setup is similar. There is a null hypothesis, which
    is the default position: the defendant is inoccent. Some strong
    evidence is needed to overturn this and convict. If the defendant
    is actually innocent and found innocent, great. If the defendant
    is actually guilty and found guilty, great. If the defendant is
    wrongfully convicted, innocent but found guilty, that is a type 1
    error, a false positive. If the defendant is guilty but found
    innocent, that is a type 2 error, a false negative. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Why go through the bother of classifying these errors? Well,
    different errors have different risks associated with them. When
    looking at a research study, you can ask yourself where the major
    risks are.
    <ul>
      <li>
        Sometimes, the false positive is a major risk. If you are
        testing a medication that has significant side effects, then
        having that medication prescirbed when it doesn't actully work
        is a major risk: all those dangerous side effects would happen
        with no justification or benefit. The false positive is the
        greater risk, so you would want to minimize type 1 errors. 
      </li>
      <li>
        Sometimes, the false negative is a major risk. If you are
        testing a food addative that might cause cancer, a false
        negative means that you think there is no cancer risk where
        there is, in fact, a risk. You end up approving the additive
        and causing a bunch of cancer cases<mdash/>obviously a very
        bad outcome. In this study, you would want to minimize type 2
        errors.
      </li>
    </ul>
    So how do we manage this? How can we design our statistics to
    favour a certain kind of errors? In hypothesis testing, we do have
    one major choice to make: the threshold of significance. (Recall
    we used the symbol <m>\alpha</m> for theshold of significance,
    which we will do in this section as well. 
  </p>
  <p>
    <m>\alpha = 0.05</m> is a standard: a middle of the road threshold
    of significant which is frequently used.  What if we want to
    specifically avoid type 1 errors? Well, type 1 is a false
    positive: we find something significant but, in truth, there isn't
    actually a significant result to find. To avoid this, we have to
    make significance harder to justify. So we lower <m>\alpha</m>. Lower
    <m>\alpha</m> means that the <m>p</m> value has to be even lower,
    which is harder to accomplish. More samples will be rejected, and
    we will have less change of calling something significant when it
    actually isn't.
  </p>
  <p>
    On the flip side, what if we want to avoid type 2 errors? Type 2
    is a false negative: we find no significance, but there actually
    is something significant to find. To avoid this, we have to make
    significance easier to justify. So we raise <m>\alpha</m>. Higher
    <m>\alpha</m> means that more <m>p</m>-values are treated as
    significant, we accept more samples, and we are less like to miss
    observing a real effect. 
  </p>
  <p>
    All of this setting of threshold of significance happens without
    actually knowing the truth of the population, of course. We never
    know the truth of the population, we only even known our sample
    and speculate about its meaning. This isn't about knowing truth;
    it's about managing risk. What is more dangeous: a false negative
    or a false positive? This is what every researcher has to consider
    when setting a threshold of significance. 
  </p>
  <p>
    In <xref ref="subsection-sided-tests"/>, I talked about one-sided
    and two sided tests. At the end of that section, I made the
    observation that moving from a one-sided to a two-sided test
    doubles <m>p</m>-values. My discussion in this section is about
    threshold of significant, so this effect of doubling is quite
    relevant. Often one-sided or two-sided tests are indeed chosen
    because of some reasonable intuitition or prediction in the data.
    However, they also can be considered for their effect on errors. Let
    me briefly summarize. 
    <ul>
      <li>
        A one-sided test has a lower <m>p</m>-value: one half of that
        of a two-sided test. A smaller <m>p</m>-value is good for
        concluding significance. Therefore, a one-sided test may lead
        to more false positives, more type 1 errors. 
      </li>
      <li>
        A two=-sided test has a higher <m>p</m>-value: double that of
        a one-sided test. A larger <m>p</m>-value is bad for
        concluding significance. Therefore, a two-sided test may lead
        to more false negative, more type 2 errors. 
      </li>
    </ul>
  </p>
  <p>
    It is generally considered good practice to default to two-sided
    tests. It is always possible that the assumption about the
    direction of an effect is wrong: we try to see if a medication is
    effect, but it turns out to be counter-effective. We assume
    certain trees grow better in wetter soils, but it might turn out
    that drier soils actually give better outcomes. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Based on this argument, a one-sided test should only be justified
    by a very strong rationale that the effect must only occur on one
    side of the data. Without a strong rationale like this, two-sided
    tests are the preferred method. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Finally, let me cover one last influence on error: sample size. In
    general, larger samples sizes are always preferred. They lead to more
    precise statistics, smaller errors. You can see this very clearly
    with the calculation for standard error. As a reminder, we've had
    two calculations so far for standard error, one for hypothesis
    testing and one for confidence intervals. Here are those two
    formula for reference.
    <md>
      SE = \sqrt{P(1-P) \left( \frac{1}{n_1} + \frac{1}{n_2} \right)}
    </md>
    <md>
      SE = \sqrt{\frac{p_1(1-p_1)}{n_1} + \frac{p_2(1-p_2)}{n_2}} 
    </md>
    The important observation here is that the sample sizes,
    <m>n_1</m> and <m>n_2</m>, are always in the denominator. We will
    see many different standard error calculations in future sections,
    but sample size will remain in the denominator. Let me run though
    the chain of reasoning this produces. 
    <ul>
      <li>
        The sample size is in the denominator. Therefore, a large
        sample size is a large denominator.
      </li>
      <li>
        Dividing by large number produces small numbers. Therefore, a
        large sample size means a small standard error. 
      </li>
      <li>
        A small standard error means a tightly clusted sampling
        distribution. Since a <m>p</m>-value is determined by the are
        of the sampling distribution out past a certain point, a
        tightly clustered sampling distribution means smaller
        <m>p</m>-value.
      </li>
      <li>
        All together, larger sample sizes means smaller <m>p</m>-value
        and, therefore, greater chance of significance, of rejecting
        the null hypothesis and supporting the alternative hypothesis. 
      </li>
    </ul>
    So larger sample sizes are more precise, lead to smaller
    <m>p</m>-value and smaller confidence intervals. All of this, in
    general, helps to reject a null hypothesis. Therefore, larger
    sample sizes are particularly helpful for avoiding type 2 errors:
    false negative. If you are worried about type 2 errors, then large
    sample sizes are particularly helpful. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Let me make one more observation about sample sizes here. The
    standard error calculations, and therefore this whole logic about
    significance and errors, depend on the sample size. They do not
    depend on the population size at all. In fact, population size
    never shows up anywhere in statistical calculation. If I have a
    sample size of <m>1000</m> cases, that's all I need to know. The
    population might be ten thousand total individuals, in which case
    my cases represent <m>10\%</m> of the population. Or the
    population might be ten miillion total individual, in which case
    my cases represent <m>0.01\%</m> of the population. Intuitively,
    it feels that measuing <m>10\%</m> of the population should be
    more valuable than measuring <m>0.01\%</m> of the population. But
    the mathematics doesn't care at all. The population size doesn't
    matter to any of this disucssion. 
  </p>
  <p>
    Finally, let me define one last term which is a useful term for
    this discussion. 
  </p>
  <definition>
    <statement>
      <p>
        A statistical test is called <term>powerful</term> if it will
        often reject the null hypothesis. 
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>
  <p>
    This is a bit vague, but you can think of a powerful test as a
    test that will avoid type 2 errors. If you want to avoid type 2
    errors, you want a more powerful test. If you want to avoid type 1
    errors, you want a less powerful test. And, mostly importantly
    here, large sample sizes increase the power of a test. 
  </p>
</section>
